  ]
)
<Fig3>

Considering the $R_(upright("AHE"))$ originates from the combination between AHE conductance $sigma_(upright("AHE"))$ and longitudinal resistivity $rho_(x x)$, we measured the $rho_(x x)$ of two remnant states and estimated the corresponded $sigma_(upright("AHE"))$ at various temperatures according to $sigma_(upright("AHE")) = rho_(upright("AHE")) \/ \( rho_(upright("AHE"))^2 + rho_(x x)^2 \)$, where $rho_(upright("AHE")) = R_(upright("AHE")) t$ and $t =$ 3 nm is the thickness of the Pt layer. See detailed data on temperature-dependent AHE results in #link(<SF6>)[10], Supplementary Materials. The calculated $sigma_(upright("AHE"))$ are shown in #link(<Fig1>)[1]\(d). At RT, the $sigma_(upright("AHE"))$ for the $P_arrow.t$ and $P_arrow.b$ states is approximately 28 and 20 $upright(Omega^(- 1) med m^(- 1))$, respectively, corresponding to 33% modulation of $sigma_(upright("AHE"))$ at RT. In the whole temperature range from 300 to 120 K, for $P_arrow.t$ state, a local maximum value of $sigma_(upright("AHE"))$ approximately 32 $upright(Omega^(- 1) med m^(- 1))$ is observed around 240 K, while a local maximum value of 33 $upright(Omega^(- 1) med m^(- 1))$ is observed around 180 K for $P_arrow.b$ state. These results suggest that the AFM strength of ultrathin NiO films is effectively modulated by $E_(upright("gate"))$.



To confirm the modulation of the AFM strength of ultrathin NiO film by $E_(upright("gate"))$, we utilized synchrotron-based XMLD measurements at the Ni $L_2$ edge using the same method described in Refs. @alders1998prb@altieri2009prb. The experimental set-up is schematically shown in #link(<Fig2>)[2]\(a), as the X-ray is incident in (010) plane with an angle of 30$""^compose$ with respect to the sample surface. The polarization vector $arrow(E)$ of X-ray can be modulated with angle $phi$ ($phi = 0^compose$ or $90^compose$) to the (010) plane. The $L_2$ intensity ratio in the X-ray absorption spectrum (XAS) at the Ni $L_2$ edge, defined as the ratio of the lower to higher energy peak intensities, is affected by the alignment of $arrow(E)$ with the Ni$""^(2 +)$ spin easy axis @stohr1999images and exhibits linear dichroism. The linear dichroism effect can arise from the XMLD effect due to AFM order and the crystal field effect; however, the latter can be disregarded since our observations show an insignificant peak energy shift at the $L_3$ edge (see #link(<SF7>)[11] in Supplementary Materials), which also exclude the Ni defects in Pt (see #link(<SF8>)[12] in Supplementary Materials). By varying the angle $phi$, the $L_2$ intensity ratio of the normalized XAS signal could change and show the XMLD effect, which indicates the AFM order strength @altieri2009prb. In this work, we define the XMLD signal = XAS$\( phi = 0^compose \) - upright("XAS") \( phi = 90^compose \)$. The typical normalized XAS results with $phi = 0^compose$ and $phi = 90^compose$ and the corresponding XMLD signal for $P_arrow.t$ and $P_arrow.b$ states are shown in #link(<Fig2>)[2]\(b) and #link(<Fig2>)[2]\(c), respectively. For both states, the $L_2$ higher energy peak intensity always reaches its maximum value (or the minimum $L_2$ intensity ratio) with $phi = 90^compose$, indicating the Ni$""^(2 +)$ spin easy axis lying in the sample plane and consistent with the reported easy-plane AFM structure of NiO thin film @wu2008analysis@gray2019spin@baldrati2018full.

Then we utilized the XMLD signal to reveal the AFM order strength modulated by $E_(upright("gate"))$. The XMLD is significant for $P_arrow.t$ state, while it becomes weaker for $P_arrow.b$ state at 300 K, see #link(<Fig2>)[2]\(b), supporting our previous demonstration of the non-volatile modulation on AFM order strength by $E_(upright("gate"))$ according to AHE measurements in #link(<Fig1>)[1]. In addition, we also conducted XAS measurements with temperatures ranging from 300 K to 200 K, and obtained temperature-dependent XMLD results, as shown in #link(<Fig2>)[2]\(c). For $P_arrow.t$ state, the XMLD signal remains significant as temperature decreases from 300 K to 200 K. We note that the XMLD signal shows a non-monotonic trend, as it becomes relatively weaker at 200 K than at 250 K, which can be ascribed to the AFM spin reorientation at low temperature @zhu2014antiferromagnetic@wu2006tailoring. Furthermore, the XMLD signal from the $P_arrow.b$ state shows a similar non-monotonic trend but remains less than the XMLD signal magnitude from the $P_arrow.t$ state. This trend aligns with the AHE results in #link(<Fig1>)[1]\(d), showing that the AFM order strength of $P_arrow.b$ state is weaker than that in $P_arrow.t$ state, which is also consistent with the reported correlation between AHE and AFM order strength in AFM conductors @nakatsuji2015large@liu2018electrical@nagaosa2010anomalous.

As a relaxor ferroelectric with significant piezoelectric effect, after applying reverse $E_(upright("gate"))$, the observed non-volatile control of AHE could be a combination of ferroelectric polarization switching and piezoelectric strain effect. The PMN--PT has a rhombohedral crystal structure, with the ferroelectric polarization of PMN--PT along $⟨111⟩$ directions in pseudo-cubic notation @chen2019giant@yan2019piezoelectric. Thus, a critical reverse $E_(upright("gate"))$ applied along the \[001\] direction can lead to 71$""^compose$, 109$""^compose$ and 180$""^compose$ switching of ferroelectric polarization @yang2014bipolar. Although saturation $E_(upright("gate"))$ favors 180$""^compose$ switching and volatile in-plane strain after removing the electric field, minor 71$""^compose$ or 109$""^compose$ switching can also remain and induce non-volatile strain that modulates the AFM order @ba2021electric. To clarify this issue, we measured the in-plane piezoelectric strain ($epsilon$) by using a strain gauge with continuous measurements on PMN--PT(100) substrate, see schematic diagram in #link(<Fig3>)[3]\(a). The in-plane piezoelectric strain as a sweep $E_(upright("gate"))$ along the \[001\] direction is displayed in #link(<Fig3>)[3]\(b), showing a butterfly-shaped strain hysteresis loop with an asymmetry component. By defining the piezoelectric strain coefficient $gamma = lr(|d epsilon \/ d E_(upright("gate"))|)$, we found the $gamma_arrow.t = 0.0063 med upright(% med c m \/ k V)$ for $P_arrow.t$ state, and $gamma_arrow.b = 0.0058 med upright(% med c m \/ k V)$ for $P_arrow.b$ state, respectively. Two non-volatile states with in-plane strain about $- 0.01 %$ corresponding to $P_arrow.t$ state and about 0% corresponding to $P_arrow.b$ state were observed after removing $E_(upright("gate"))$. Next, to clarify the dominant effect in the non-volatile control of AFM order, we studied the piezoelectric response of AHE. The polarization is held upward for $P_arrow.t$ state and downward for $P_arrow.b$ state, respectively, and the $E_(upright("gate"))$ is applied $i n$-$s i t u$ to the device up to $plus.minus 4$ kV/cm; see detailed raw data in #link(<SF9>)[13], Supplementary Materials. The results are shown in #link(<Fig3>)[3]\(c) and #link(<Fig3>)[3]\(d), displaying similar magnitude of magnetoelectric coupling coefficient $beta = d upright("R")_(upright("AHE")) \/ d E_(upright("gate"))$ for both states. We found $beta_arrow.t = 0.31 med upright(m Omega med c m \/ k V)$ for $P_arrow.t$ state and $beta_arrow.b = 0.44 med upright(m Omega med c m \/ k V)$ for $P_arrow.b$ state. The increase of in-plane compressive strain gives rise to enhancement of $R_(upright("AHE"))$, which can be understood as the enhancement of AFM interaction $J_(upright("ex"))$ by reducing interatomic spacing @liu2018electrical. However, the magnitude of $R_(upright("AHE"))$ of $P_arrow.b$ state is always lower than that of $P_arrow.t$ state in the range of applied $E_(upright("gate"))$. Considering the strain difference of about 0.01% between $P_arrow.t$ and $P_arrow.b$, the pure modulation of $R_(upright("AHE"))$ originates from the remnant strain can be calculated through $Delta R_(upright("AHE"))^(upright("strain")) = frac(d R_(upright("AHE")), d epsilon) Delta epsilon = frac(d R_(upright("AHE")), d E_(upright("gate"))) dot.op frac(d E_(upright("gate")), d epsilon) Delta epsilon = beta \/ gamma dot.op Delta epsilon$. Using the coefficiencies from experimental results, we estimated the $Delta R_(upright("AHE"))^(upright("strain"))$ is about 0.63$plus.minus$0.14~m$Omega$, which can only contribute to about 25% of the non-volatile modulation of the $R_(upright("AHE"))$ in #link(<Fig1>)[1]\(c). Thus, in our system, the field effect imposed by ferroelectric polarization of PMN--PT mainly contributes to the non-volatile control of AFM order strength and corresponding $R_(upright("AHE"))$.

Due to the bound charge carried by ferroelectric polarization, electron/hole doping into the ultrathin NiO layer could happen for $P_arrow.t$/$P_arrow.b$ states @gu2024ferroelectric@song2017recent@si2019ferroelectric. In correlated magnetic materials, this doping usually results in modulation of $J_(upright("ex"))$ @zheng2018ambipolar, as schematically shown in #link(<Fig4>)[4]\(a). To qualitatively understand the impact of hole/electron doping on $J_(upright("ex"))$ in the NiO layer, we conducted theoretical calculations based on density functional theory (DFT) to investigate $J_(e x)$. The number of doping charge per unit cell ($N$) for either electron or hole doping is simply estimated by $N = P V_0 \/ d e approx med 0.07$, where $P approx 20 med mu upright("C") \/ upright("cm")^2$ is the magnitude of ferroelectric polarization, $V_0$ is the volume of the NiO unit cell, $d approx med 1.2 med upright("nm")$ is the thickness of the NiO layer, and $e$ is the electron charge. Thus, we calculated $J_(upright("ex"))$ considering $N$ from 0 to 0.1 with electron or hole doping. The NiO remains insulating according to the band structure, see details in #link(<SF10>)[14], Supplementary Materials. Without doping, $J_(upright("ex"))$ is calculated to be about $- 9.3$ meV, located in the reasonable range within previous theoretical studies @PhysRevB.66.064434@PhysRevB.65.155102. Furthermore, our DFT calculations revealed that both hole and electron doping in the NiO layer lead to a weakening of the $J_(upright("ex"))$. However, this modulation depends on the type of doping and shows strong asymmetry, which has been intensively demonstrated in correlated materials @zheng2018ambipolar@parschke2017correlation@segawa2010zero. The $J_(upright("ex"))$ of electron doping is always larger than hole doping, giving rise to stronger AFM strength probed by larger AHE at RT (#link(<Fig1>)[1]), as well as a stronger XMLD signal (#link(<Fig2>)[2]). The DFT calculation is further supported by a Pt(3 nm)/NiO(4 u.c.)/PMN-PT control sample (see #link(<SF11>)[15] in Supplementary Materials), in which the nonvolatile modulation of AHE resistance is much smaller due to thicker NiO, aligning with the scenario of the ferroelectric field-effect.

Our results highlight that the AFM state can be effectively probed through interfacial effects using the AHE, despite the spin-independent nature of bulk AFMs that normally preserves spin degeneracy. Although demonstrated here with insulating NiO, similar phenomena could be extended to conducting AFMs through precise thickness control, opening new avenues for designing functional interface-engineered AFM devices @YANG2025100142@zhao2025collinearantiferromagnetictunneljunctions. A possible asymmetric spin--orbit torque driven AFM switching may also emerge with an appropriate crystalline orientation under a piezoelectric strain--modulated energy barrier governed by the AFM strength @zhang2025deterministicswitchingneelvector.

